@@ -0,0 +1,122 @@
+# Host permit-renewal coalescing model
+
+Run from the AOSP root:
+
+```sh
+python3 -B system/core/libplatform_integrity_host/tests/lease_model/test_model.py
+```
+
+This is an isolated sequential specification, not part of Android.bp. It performs
+no native compilation, device I/O, signing, property mutation or real shutdown.
+Running this model does not change production state. A candidate implementation
+now exists in the Framework host (`PermitRenewal` / `PlatformIntegrityService`),
+with 13 separate Java helper tests prepared for maintainer compilation. Existing
+query intervals remain unchanged; the Python tests do not execute that Java code.
+
+## Scope and source anchors
+
+The candidate change concerns only redundant `sys.integrity.policy` writes in
+`frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/platformintegrity/PlatformIntegrityService.java`.
+`publishLocked()` currently writes a four-second lease on each successful cycle;
+the cycle's existing 500ms scheduling, environment/control queries, proc publication
+and final receipt/ticket validation are separate work and must stay in place.
+
+The existing source contracts also include:
+
+- `EnvironmentSnapshot` and `AsterNotification.Gate`: report/receipt freshness
+  under two seconds, exact identities and generations, final ticket recheck.
+- `android.os.PlatformIntegrity` and the host `integrity_policy.h`: consumer
+  expiry is checked against BOOTTIME; a local host denial cannot erase a permit
+  already delivered through the property.
+- `ShutdownPermitWriter` / `ShutdownGate`: forced bounded revocation, actual
+  readback and one-way shutdown denial. These must bypass normal coalescing.
+
+The proposed margin is **two seconds remaining on the existing four-second lease**.
+It is a model fixture, not an accepted new runtime constant. Nothing raises the
+four-second issue lifetime or existing five-second parser guard.
+
+## Decision contract
+
+Every modeled cycle still requires externally validated current environment,
+control-chain and coordinated publication proof. It cannot refresh a report's
+completed time, reuse a superseded receipt, bypass a missing inventory read or
+make a V4 early-name row verified. Feature/mode selection is supplied by the
+existing host policy; this model never decides that Glow or any other environment
+qualifies for a feature.
+
+Only a positive, unchanged, still-valid permit may skip a write, and only when:
+
+1. The actual property equals this coordinator's last **successfully written**
+   value, including deadline, mask and identity; cached desired features alone
+   are insufficient.
+2. The current proof, notification/revision/identity generation and control expiry
+   are valid at the decision and at the final check.
+3. Remaining lifetime is strictly greater than the renewal margin. The exact
+   margin boundary performs a write.
+
+A skip changes neither actual deadline nor last-write success state. An unknown
+startup value is never adopted as a successful cached write. Property replacement
+retires the proof and forces zero; later positive recovery needs fresh validation.
+Mask/identity changes, explicit force, invalidation, expiration and shutdown never
+coalesce. Zero writes are deliberately not optimized in this first model.
+
+Both the write and skip branches need late-result checks. A notice, property
+replacement, shutdown or expiry during either branch cannot commit host authority.
+Forced zero must use the existing publication serialization. Failure cannot become
+cached success, advance an alleged successful deadline or revive an old result.
+
+The model conservatively freezes positive issuance after writer/readback failure,
+as the current host's writer-error path does. Failed cleanup may leave an already
+issued property valid until its original expiry. Tests distinguish local host
+denial from what an execution-side consumer can still read; they do not pretend
+that a failed zero write revoked external authority.
+
+## Explicit tradeoffs
+
+- **Less availability headroom:** after several skipped writes, a coordinator
+  stall or suspend can leave less time on the last issued permit. Functions may
+  become temporarily unavailable sooner than with rewriting every 500ms. Expired
+  authority requires a fresh generation/validation; no fallback extends it.
+- **Less frequent implicit writer probing:** an unchanged-value skip does not
+  exercise the property-service setter. A write-channel fault can remain unseen
+  until the next due renewal or forced revocation, instead of the next old-style
+  periodic write. Environment/control queries are unchanged, and no permit lives
+  longer because of this; nevertheless it is an operational diagnostic difference.
+- **More state/race handling:** successful-value tracking, unexpected replacement,
+  both-branch rechecks and shutdown serialization must be implemented correctly.
+- **Limited benefit:** under a nominal stable 500ms cycle, a ten-second fixture
+  gives 20 real writes without coalescing versus five with it. Both perform 20
+  validation cycles. This is ~75% fewer writes for one property, not fewer timers,
+  RPCs, proc publications or a measured CPU/battery improvement.
+
+No new suspend-prevention mechanism, wake lock or alarm is proposed. BOOTTIME
+advances during synthetic suspend, and proof/lease expiry is checked before work
+can restore authority. No power, actual scheduler latency or resume safety proof
+follows from the synthetic tests.
+
+## Model boundaries and next implementation requirements
+
+`Proof.validated` is a fixture for the existing complete successful query and
+receipt validation. This model does not implement the socket protocol, baseline
+verification, mode state machine, property format, Android permissions, raw
+collector parser, framework locks or memory ordering. `generation` combines
+identity/revision/notice fencing abstractly; existing epoch changes, endpoint
+rebinding and cache invalidation remain required in production. It is not a new
+wire field. Python integers do not prove fixed-width counter exhaustion behavior.
+
+`before` and `after` hooks represent serialized adversarial boundaries, not real
+concurrent threads or successful proof of atomic system-wide execution. Last-write
+and actual-property comparisons must be placed under the appropriate existing
+locks, and the actual property can still change after the final comparison. Do
+not promise zero-window rollback of delivered results or in-flight effects.
+
+Before integration, preserve forced zero publication for shutdown, historical
+alerts, pending/failed evidence, notification interruption and mode transitions.
+Do not move successful-write bookkeeping ahead of setter success or skip the
+post-decision generation check. The model's fresh-generation recovery on expiry
+is conservative; runtime integration must retain the host's real identity/rebind
+semantics rather than copying the synthetic generation directly.
+
+TEE heartbeat/ACK scheduling, the two-second report limit, three-second proc
+expiry, full evidence/control queries and FAS execution optimization are outside
+this change. No historical anomaly is cleared.
